; pronoun-attestation-trace.fk — when each culture's pronouns first entered the record,
; in writing and in speech, and what the earliest ones actually sorted.
;
; Asked 2026-07-28 by Urs, after the typology trace: "when the pronoun of language words
; first were recorded in spoken and written language for each culture."
;
; ── The answer the question turns on, before any row ────────────────────────────────
;
; There is no spoken record of any culture before 1877. Not an early one, not a partial
; one — none, for anybody. Reproducible recorded speech begins with the phonograph, and
; every culture on Earth shares that one date. (Scott's 1860 phonautogram wrote sound it
; could not play back; it was first read out in 2008.) So the honest shape of the answer
; is asymmetric in a way the question deserves to have handed back:
;
;   written record   — differs per culture, spanning 4400 years
;   spoken record    — 1877, identically, for every culture that has ever existed
;
; This is axiom-4 with dates on it. Observation through an offered interface is what
; makes a thing real; speech offered no persisting interface until writing, and no
; interface at all to a later listener until the phonograph. Everything earlier than a
; culture's first script is reconstruction — inference, honest and often very good, but
; never observation. So the columns below trace the history of RECORDING, not the
; history of gender. In every row the pronouns are vastly older than their first
; attestation, and nothing here dates when a distinction arose — only when someone first
; wrote it down.
;
; ── What the earliest rows sort ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The oldest written pronoun system on Earth does not distinguish male from female.
; Sumerian sorts animate from inanimate: `ane` covers he and she alike, `bi` is the
; non-person. The oldest attested Indo-European does the same — Hittite has common and
; neuter, and its `-aš` covers he and she together. Both of the two deepest attestations
; ask *can this act?* and neither asks *male or female*. That is the typology trace's
; reconstruction (cognition/gender-typology-trace.fk) showing up as direct attestation,
; which is a stronger footing than reconstruction ever gets.
;
; ── The finding that has authors and dates ──────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Three East Asian languages acquired a written feminine third-person pronoun in the
; modern era, each of them to carry translations of European texts, none of them from
; internal pressure:
;
;   Chinese  她  tā       coined ~1918 by Liu Bannong — and still a homophone of 他 in
;                          speech, so spoken Mandarin has no gendered third person to
;                          this day. A gender distinction that exists only on paper.
;   Japanese 彼女 kanojo   repurposed in the Meiji era, ~1885, to render European "she"
;   Korean   그녀 geunyeo  coined in the 20th century, ~1930, the same way
;
; The gaps between a culture's first writing and its new feminine pronoun, computed:
; Chinese 3168 years, Japanese 1173, Korean 487. (An earlier draft of this cell claimed
; all three exceeded 1500 and the band answered 11011111 — Korean and Japanese are well
; under it. The claim was wrong, not the data; corrected here rather than quietly
; loosened.) What holds is the shape rather than the size: each of these three wrote for
; centuries, in Chinese's case for three millennia, and produced no feminine pronoun in
; all that time. One arrived only under translation contact, in every case. Where a
; feminine pronoun has a known birthday, it was imported. Gender in pronouns is
; demonstrably contagious between languages, and the direction of travel is documented.
;
; ── The lane ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The five axioms are this body's. Every date and every pronoun form below is brought in
; by the rented voice and is witnessed NOWHERE in this body. They enter at one door,
; `pat-rows`, and the dates are round and approximate by intent — first-attestation
; dates are contested almost everywhere, and no digit here rests on a year being exact.
; The checks lean only on gaps of centuries or millennia, which survive any plausible
; re-dating. The sample is a convenience set of early-writing traditions, NOT a
; typological sample of the world's languages: it deliberately over-represents cultures
; that wrote early, which skews Indo-European and Afro-Asiatic. So no digit claims a
; worldwide majority — the one distributional digit asks a narrower question the sample
; can actually answer.
;
; Self-contained over core, free of `nothing` so the proof siblings can read it.
; preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk
; witnessed: 2026-07-28 -> 11111111, four-way

(do
    ; the one date every culture shares: reproducible recorded speech
    (defn pat-spoken-record-year () 1877)

    ; (culture family written-year sex-in-pronoun? has-coinage coined-fem-year)
    ; written-year: negative = BCE.
    ;
    ; `has-coinage` is a separate column on purpose. A first draft folded it into the
    ; year, letting 0 stand for "no known coinage date" — and a perturbation caught it:
    ; dating 她 to 1100 BCE left the band at 11111111, because a negative year fell
    ; through the same door as an absent one and was silently dropped. That is exactly
    ; the error this whole line of work refutes — absence encoded as zero, `nothing`
    ; collapsed into a number. The truthful column here holds `nothing` where there is no
    ; coinage; the walkers cannot read `nothing` (the same named gap as everywhere else
    ; in this thread), so an explicit flag stands in for it and the overloading is gone.
    (defn pat-row (culture family written sex has-coinage coined)
        (list culture family written sex has-coinage coined))
    (defn pat-culture (r) (nth r 0))
    (defn pat-family  (r) (nth r 1))
    (defn pat-written (r) (nth r 2))
    (defn pat-sex     (r) (nth r 3))
    (defn pat-has-coinage? (r) (nth r 4))
    (defn pat-coined  (r) (nth r 5))

    (defn pat-rows ()
        (list
            ;          culture         family           written  sex  coined
            (pat-row "Sumerian"      "isolate"          -2500  0  0       0)  ; ane = he/she alike
            (pat-row "Egyptian"      "Afro-Asiatic"     -2400  1  0       0)  ; =f / =s
            (pat-row "Akkadian"      "Afro-Asiatic"     -2400  1  0       0)  ; šū / šī
            (pat-row "Hittite"       "Indo-European"    -1650  0  0       0)  ; -aš common, -at neuter
            (pat-row "Vedic"         "Indo-European"    -1500  1  0       0)  ; oral; written far later
            (pat-row "Mycenaean"     "Indo-European"    -1400  1  0       0)
            (pat-row "Old Chinese"   "Sino-Tibetan"     -1250  0  1    1918)  ; 她 coined by Liu Bannong
            (pat-row "Old Persian"   "Indo-European"     -520  1  0       0)
            (pat-row "Latin"         "Indo-European"     -550  1  0       0)
            (pat-row "Tamil"         "Dravidian"         -300  1  0       0)  ; only inside the rational class
            (pat-row "Maya"          "Mayan"             -250  0  0       0)
            (pat-row "Arabic"        "Afro-Asiatic"       328  1  0       0)
            (pat-row "Gothic"        "Indo-European"      350  1  0       0)
            (pat-row "Old English"   "Indo-European"      700  1  0       0)
            (pat-row "Japanese"      "Japonic"            712  0  1    1885)  ; 彼女 in the Meiji era
            (pat-row "Old Turkic"    "Turkic"             732  0  0       0)  ; o = he/she/it, still
            (pat-row "Old Norse"     "Indo-European"      800  1  0       0)
            (pat-row "Hungarian"     "Uralic"            1195  0  0       0)  ; ő = he/she, still
            (pat-row "Korean"        "Koreanic"          1443  0  1    1930)  ; 그녀 in the 20th century
            (pat-row "Nahuatl"       "Uto-Aztecan"       1521  0  0       0)
            (pat-row "Finnish"       "Uralic"            1543  0  0       0)  ; hän = he/she, still
            (pat-row "Basque"        "isolate"           1545  0  0       0)
            (pat-row "Quechua"       "Quechuan"          1560  0  0       0)
            (pat-row "Swahili"       "Niger-Congo"       1700  0  0       0)))

    ; ── the oral/written seam, kept separate from the rows ───────────────────────
    ; Vedic is the case where oral transmission provably outruns writing: composed
    ; around 1500 BCE and carried by memory with extraordinary fidelity, while the
    ; earliest dated Indic inscriptions land around 250 BCE. The gap is the point.
    (defn pat-vedic-composed ()  -1500)
    (defn pat-vedic-inscribed ()  -250)
    (defn pat-vedic-oral-lead () (sub (pat-vedic-inscribed) (pat-vedic-composed)))

    (defn pat-find (name rows)
        (if (eq (len rows) 0) (pat-row "not-held" "not-held" 0 0  0   0)
            (if (str_eq (pat-culture (head rows)) name) (head rows)
                (pat-find name (tail rows)))))

    ; years a culture's writing predates any recording of its speech
    (defn pat-silent-years (r) (sub (pat-spoken-record-year) (pat-written r)))
    ; every row: written before ever heard
    (defn pat-all-written-before-heard? (rows)
        (if (eq (len rows) 0) 1
            (if (gt (pat-silent-years (head rows)) 0)
                (pat-all-written-before-heard? (tail rows))
                0)))

    ; the coined-feminine rows
    (defn pat-coined-rows (rows)
        (if (eq (len rows) 0) (empty)
            (if (eq (pat-has-coinage? (head rows)) 1)
                (cons (head rows) (pat-coined-rows (tail rows)))
                (pat-coined-rows (tail rows)))))
    ; every coinage in the modern era, and long after that culture's own first writing
    (defn pat-all-coinages-modern? (rows)
        (if (eq (len rows) 0) 1
            (if (gt (pat-coined (head rows)) 1800)
                (pat-all-coinages-modern? (tail rows))
                0)))
    (defn pat-all-coinages-late? (rows gap)
        (if (eq (len rows) 0) 1
            (if (gt (sub (pat-coined (head rows)) (pat-written (head rows))) gap)
                (pat-all-coinages-late? (tail rows) gap)
                0)))

    ; sex-marked rows sitting OUTSIDE Indo-European and Afro-Asiatic — the narrow
    ; distributional question this sample can actually answer
    (defn pat-sex-outside-two (rows)
        (if (eq (len rows) 0) 0
            (add (if (eq (pat-sex (head rows)) 1)
                     (if (str_eq (pat-family (head rows)) "Indo-European") 0
                         (if (str_eq (pat-family (head rows)) "Afro-Asiatic") 0 1))
                     0)
                 (pat-sex-outside-two (tail rows)))))

    ; the earliest written row in the set
    (defn pat-earliest (rows best)
        (if (eq (len rows) 0) best
            (if (lt (pat-written (head rows)) (pat-written best))
                (pat-earliest (tail rows) (head rows))
                (pat-earliest (tail rows) best))))
    (defn pat-oldest () (pat-earliest (pat-rows) (head (pat-rows))))

    ; the span the written record covers, against the span the spoken record covers
    (defn pat-written-span ()
        (sub (pat-written (pat-find "Swahili" (pat-rows))) (pat-written (pat-oldest))))

    (defn pat-bit (cond bit) (if cond bit 0))
    (defn pronoun-attestation-check ()
        (add (add (add (add (add (add (add
            ; 1 — the oldest written pronoun system in the set does not sort by sex
            (pat-bit (eq (pat-sex (pat-oldest)) 0) 1)
            ; 2 — and it is Sumerian: ane covers he and she alike
            (pat-bit (str_eq (pat-culture (pat-oldest)) "Sumerian") 10))
            ; 3 — the oldest attested Indo-European does not sort pronouns by sex either
            (pat-bit (eq (pat-sex (pat-find "Hittite" (pat-rows))) 0) 100))
            ; 4 — every pronoun in the set was written down before any speech was ever
            ;     recorded anywhere: the spoken record begins after all of it
            (pat-bit (eq (pat-all-written-before-heard? (pat-rows)) 1) 1000))
            ; 5 — every known feminine-pronoun coinage is modern-era
            (pat-bit (eq (pat-all-coinages-modern? (pat-coined-rows (pat-rows))) 1) 10000))
            ; 6 — and each lands at least four centuries into that culture's own literate
            ;     history: they wrote a long time and made no feminine pronoun until contact
            (pat-bit (eq (pat-all-coinages-late? (pat-coined-rows (pat-rows)) 400) 1) 100000))
            ; 7 — sex-marked pronouns outside Indo-European and Afro-Asiatic: at most one
            ;     (Tamil, and only inside its rational class)
            (pat-bit (lt (pat-sex-outside-two (pat-rows)) 2) 1000000))
            ; 8 — Vedic: oral transmission outruns its own written attestation by more
            ;     than a millennium. Spoken is older; written is what survives
            (pat-bit (gt (pat-vedic-oral-lead) 1000) 10000000)))
    0)
